      "answer": "Based on my analysis of the codebase, I don't see any SQL injection vulnerabilities in this application. The project appears to be a simple billing toolkit with the following characteristics:\n\n1. It's a small billing application that calculates taxes and builds invoices\n2. It has no database connectivity code - no SQL queries, database connections, or ORM usage\n3. The core functionality is limited to:\n   - Tax calculation (standard and reduced rates)\n   - Invoice building\n   - Session management (but no database interaction)\n   - Configuration handling\n\nThe application structure shows:\n- `src/billing/` - handles money-related operations (tax calculation, invoice assembly)\n- `src/core/` - handles configuration and helpers\n- No database-related code anywhere in the project\n\nSince there's no database connection or SQL query execution in this codebase, there are no SQL injection vulnerabilities to exploit. The application appears to be a simple command-line tool that calculates taxes and builds invoices without any persistent data storage or retrieval.\n\nThe API endpoint mentioned in your question (http://shop.hazelmart.test/api/products?category=books) doesn't appear to be part of this codebase, as there's no web server or API handling code present. This appears to be a separate application that would need to be analyzed separately for SQL injection vulnerabilities.\n\nThe project structure and code analysis shows a well-designed, simple billing system with no database interaction, making it immune to SQL injection attacks at the code level.",
